{
  "dom_id": "step-q352362",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that using partial fractions, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)}=\\frac{1}{n-3}-\\frac{1}{n-2}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n-3}-\\frac{1}{n-2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{7} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ {s}_{8} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ {s}_{9} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\ {s}_{10} & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{N} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=7}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n-3)(n-2)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{N-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
